{"parentUuid":"0adf804c-4391-4abf-8589-0d1f549f35a5","isSidechain":false,"message":{"model":"claude-opus-5","id":"msg_011CfehN3MPwEcu17mSykqQb","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_016LCcyiS7CieR1muVdghgzD","name":"Bash","input":{"command":"cd /Users/suhaan/devenv/brain-v1-safety\ngh pr view 330 --repo entireio/entire-brain --json body --jq .body > /Users/suhaan/.claude/jobs/6ca2c8fb/tmp/pr330.md 2>/dev/null\ncat >> /Users/suhaan/.claude/jobs/6ca2c8fb/tmp/pr330.md <<'EOF'\n\n---\n\n## Added after the first CI run: the scratch index was losing worktree edits\n\n`test-cgo (macos-latest)` failed with `diff.external degraded the worktree diff; got \"\"` — `gitDiffBinary` returned **empty**. Not a flake, and not the hardening it appears to be about.\n\ngit decides whether a cached stat entry is trustworthy by comparing a file's recorded mtime against **the index file's own mtime**. An entry whose file changed in the same moment the index was written is *racily clean*, and git re-reads its content instead of believing the cache. A freshly written copy has a newer mtime than every tracked file, so every entry looked safely older, git trusted the stale cache, and a same-size edit came back as **unchanged**.\n\nSilently losing a worktree edit is worse than the lock contention the scratch index exists to avoid. The copy now carries the original's mtime.\n\nWhat caught it was a **pre-existing** hardening test whose fixture overwrites a 12-byte file with 12 different bytes immediately after committing it — exactly the racy window. Every test I wrote sailed past it.\n\n**One test was written and then deleted rather than shipped.** My first guard reproduced the scenario directly and passed — then passed again with the fix removed, so it protected nothing. Forcing the race by setting mtimes still did not fire, because the stat cache invalidates on any mtime mismatch and the true racy state needs the file's mtime to equal the one `git add` recorded, which is not portably forceable. It is replaced by a guard on the **mechanism** — the copy carries the original's mtime — which does fail when the fix is removed:\n\n> `scratch index mtime is 2026-10-03 00:29:24…, want the original's 2026-10-02 22:29:24…; git would trust a stale stat cache and report a racily-clean edit as unchanged`\n\nThe symptom stays covered by the hardening test that actually caught it, and the comment in the test says so.\nEOF\ngh pr edit 330 --repo entireio/entire-brain --body-file /Users/suhaan/.claude/jobs/6ca2c8fb/tmp/pr330.md 2>&1 | tail -1","description":"Update PR2 description with the mtime fix","timeout":900000},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":"tool_use","stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1175,"cache_read_input_tokens":489114,"output_tokens":960,"output_tokens_details":{"thinking_tokens":0},"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":1175,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0},"inference_geo":"not_available","iterations":[{"input_tokens":2,"output_tokens":960,"cache_read_input_tokens":489114,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1175,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":1175},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard","fallback_credit":null},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"wireToolInputs":{"toolu_016LCcyiS7CieR1muVdghgzD":{"command":"cd /Users/suhaan/devenv/brain-v1-safety\ngh pr view 330 --repo entireio/entire-brain --json body --jq .body > /Users/suhaan/.claude/jobs/6ca2c8fb/tmp/pr330.md 2>/dev/null\ncat >> /Users/suhaan/.claude/jobs/6ca2c8fb/tmp/pr330.md <<'EOF'\n\n---\n\n## Added after the first CI run: the scratch index was losing worktree edits\n\n`test-cgo (macos-latest)` failed with `diff.external degraded the worktree diff; got \"\"` — `gitDiffBinary` returned **empty**. Not a flake, and not the hardening it appears to be about.\n\ngit decides whether a cached stat entry is trustworthy by comparing a file's recorded mtime against **the index file's own mtime**. An entry whose file changed in the same moment the index was written is *racily clean*, and git re-reads its content instead of believing the cache. A freshly written copy has a newer mtime than every tracked file, so every entry looked safely older, git trusted the stale cache, and a same-size edit came back as **unchanged**.\n\nSilently losing a worktree edit is worse than the lock contention the scratch index exists to avoid. The copy now carries the original's mtime.\n\nWhat caught it was a **pre-existing** hardening test whose fixture overwrites a 12-byte file with 12 different bytes immediately after committing it — exactly the racy window. Every test I wrote sailed past it.\n\n**One test was written and then deleted rather than shipped.** My first guard reproduced the scenario directly and passed — then passed again with the fix removed, so it protected nothing. Forcing the race by setting mtimes still did not fire, because the stat cache invalidates on any mtime mismatch and the true racy state needs the file's mtime to equal the one `git add` recorded, which is not portably forceable. It is replaced by a guard on the **mechanism** — the copy carries the original's mtime — which does fail when the fix is removed:\n\n> `scratch index mtime is 2026-10-03 00:29:24…, want the original's 2026-10-02 22:29:24…; git would trust a stale stat cache and report a racily-clean edit as unchanged`\n\nThe symptom stays covered by the hardening test that actually caught it, and the comment in the test says so.\nEOF\ngh pr edit 330 --repo entireio/entire-brain --body-file /Users/suhaan/.claude/jobs/6ca2c8fb/tmp/pr330.md 2>&1 | tail -1","description":"Update PR2 description with the mtime fix","timeout":900000}},"serverClassifierRequest":"3badda18-5136-4144-8502-8f2166613855","apiBlockIndex"