r, not a bug. There are almost no gates;\nthe only hard one is root resolution (`agent_setup.go:47`).\n\n**Two hazards in that path.** `ENTIRE_REPO_ROOT` outranks cwd (`env.go:23,73`), so a host that sets\nit can silently install into the outer repo; and the walk-up stops at the *innermost* `.git`\n(`migration.go:94-131`), so a nested clone captures the files. Also: the combined guide carrying\nthe Graph directive ships **only when both products are enabled** (`guide.go:128`) — a brain-only\ninstall gets `BrainGuide()` with no Graph instruction at all (`guide.go:130`).\n\n**Graph has no MCP server, by design.** No MCP/JSON-RPC dependency in `go.mod`, no server under\n`cmd/` or `internal/cli`, and `README.md:268` states it will not expose one. So Bash is the only\nroute to Graph, and the guide is correct to name a shell command — but an MCP-first harness has no\nGraph tool at all. This is a constraint to measure around, not a bug to fix.\n\n**The measurement already exists and is usable.** `entire graph stats` computes\n`graph_first_rate = GraphFirstSessions / SessionsWithLocate`\n(`entire-graph/internal/cli/stats.go:1183-1189,1236`) from local Claude Code JSONL transcripts in\n`~/.claude/projects/<repo-slug>/` (`:374-386`), and `--sessions-dir` / `--transcript` override that\npath — which is what makes a clean-env arm measurable. Two caveats that change the design:\n\n- **MCP calls are invisible to it.** Only Bash `entire graph <verb>` / `entire-graph <verb>` count\n  as graph calls (`:1048-1055`); `mcp__entire-brain__*` matches neither bucket. Brain usage\n  therefore needs its own measure.\n- **Pin the binary.** The withdrawn \"82.9M tokens saved\" figure was binary-version drift\n  (`scripts/entire-graph-statusline.sh:115`), and the graph-first rate runs in the same binary.\n\nThe exploration could not find the provenance of the quoted `332 / 96,987 (0.34%)` baseline — it is\nonly ever quoted, never derived. Treat it as context, not as this experiment's control.\n\n### The two unknowns that decide everything\n\nNeither is answerable from source, and both are cheap to settle:\n\n1. **Does the model actually receive the guide** through `CLAUDE.md`'s `@.entire/agent-guide.md`\n   import? If not, every wording fix in #331 is inert.\n2. **Is `$CLAUDE_FILE_PATH` populated** in a `PreToolUse` hook? #331's wiring passes\n   `--file \"$CLAUDE_FILE_PATH\"`; if that variable is not set, the hook runs with an empty path and\n   **my own hook is a no-op.**\n\nThese run first, as two single sessions, before any arm.\n\n---\n\n## Scope: entire-graph is OUT, and that is now settled\n\nThe decoy/ranking problem is the half that makes usage *persist* — 3 of 4 sessions used the tool\nexactly as instructed, got a decoy, and succeeded with grep instead. The\n`redesign-graph-cost-reduction` session answered, and it changes the picture:\n\n- **Sample-idf is already fixed, unmerged** — PR #291, also inside the launch-candidate stack\n  (#315 at `dd76b8fe`, and `0d92152d`). My earlier `internal/sem/search.go` citations were from a\n  stale build; the current code picks repo-wide DF when an exact corpus is known.\n- **I re-measured with their frozen binary and the decoy reproduces identically.**\n  `entire-graph-0d92152d` (sha256 `09f393fe…`): ranks 1–3 are\n  `brainBriefSemanticNeedsCurrentBoundary` 30.13 / `acquireSemanticIndexLock` 29.38 /\n  `runSemanticIngestTraces` 29.37 — the same scores to 2dp as the old binary, and **neither real\n  decider appears anywhere in the returned set.** So the idf fix does not move this case, which\n  isolates it to name-signal dominance rather than idf. Their follow-up sharpened it further: the\n  deciders being *absent from the returned set* makes this a **recall** failure, not merely an\n  ordering one — no reranking could rescue a candidate that was never returned.\n- **Name dominance is diagnosed, deliberately not patched ad hoc.** `searchNameCoverageWeight=10`\n  is intentionally not idf-weighted; their post-hoc study-6 found the name gap in 25/66 ranking\n  misses vs 16/123 hits, and a scoring emulator showed weight 0 rescuing 6 misses and losing 0\n  hits — emulator evidence only. The real test is a pre-registered \"study 7\" on 12 fresh repos\n  with a paraphrase stratum.\n\n**Codex holds the Go lease on entire-graph, and ranking changes go through that prereg.** So no\nentire-graph file gets touched here — tuning to my single case is exactly what the prereg exists to\nprevent, and they are right about that. Three commitments made to them and kept in this plan:\n\n1. The decoy query is **held out** — it goes into an evaluation set only, never a tuning set.\n2. The clean-env arms **pin graph to `0d92152d`**, so they measure their current state rather than\n   the stale installed symlink (which points at a dev checkout).\n3. The repro gets filed as a GitHub issue on `entireio/entire-graph` — recorded, no collision.\n\nOne finding handed back to them rather than acted on: `files_scanned=1855` but\n`files_content_read_during_preselection=1853`, with 12 `E_MINIFIED` partial failures in the same\noutput. If a deliberate skip counts as \"unread\" and thereby revokes the exact-corpus idf path, that\nis a defect in its own right. They are passing it, plus a suggestion to expose `idf_source` in\n`stats` so it is checkable from outside, to Codex.\n\n**Consequence for the bar below:** Graph ranking will still return this class of decoy during the\nexperiment. So the third pre-registered metric — used-then-abandoned — is measuring something this\nplan *cannot* fix. It is reported, not optimised, and if A1 clears rows 1–2 but fails row 3, the\nhonest conclusion is \"brain-side fix works; sustained usage is gated on graph ranking\", not a\nfailure of #331.\n\n---\n\n## Phase 1 — Harness (no model cost)\n\nBuild under `~/.claude/jobs/<job>/tmp/cleanenv/`, nothing inside `devenv/`.\n\n- **Pinned binaries.** Build `entire-brain` from the stack tip (`fix/agent-guide-directive-and-discovery`)\n  and record the commit; use the installed `entire-graph` and record its version. Every arm uses\n  the same two binaries.\n- **One fresh clone per arm**, each with no `.entire/`, no `CLAUDE.md`, no `AGENTS.md`, so arms\n  cannot contaminate each other. `ENTIRE_REPO_ROOT` explicitly unset in every arm (it outranks cwd).\n- **Instrumentation, three independent channels** — because no single one sees everything:\n  | channel | catches | why needed |\n  |---|---|---|\n  | PATH shim logging every `entire` call with a run id | all Brain + Graph **CLI** calls, in order | the only ordered record of what the agent actually ran |\n  | `entire graph stats --sessions-dir <arm>` | `graph_first_rate`, graph calls, exploration calls | the project's own metric, so before/after is comparable |\n  | transcript grep for `mcp__entire-brain__` | Brain **MCP** usage | invisible to `graph stats` |\n- **Isolated transcript dir per arm** so `--sessions-dir` reads only that arm's sessions.\n- A fixed task set (6–8 locate/explain/change tasks) written once and reused verbatim across arms.\n\n## Phase 2 — Settle the two unknowns (2 sessions)\n\n- **Probe A:** `claude -p \"quote the sentence in your instructions about your FIRST action\"` with cwd\n  in an `init-agents`-installed clone. Establishes whether the `@` import reaches the model.\n- **Probe B:** a session told to edit one file; check the shim log for a `hook pre-edit --file <path>`\n  call with a **non-empty** path. Establishes whether `$CLAUDE_FILE_PATH` works.\n\nIf Probe A fails, the fix is to inline the directive into `CLAUDE.md` rather than import it, and\neverything downstream re-runs. If Probe B fails, #331's hook wiring is corrected before the arms run.\n\n## Phase 3 — Arms, pre-registered\n\nThree arms, **n ≥ 10 sessions each**, same tasks, same binaries, fresh clone per arm:\n\n- **A0 — no guide.** `setup` only, no `init-agents`. The true baseline.\n- **A1 — guide only.** `init-agents`, hook entry removed from `.claude/settings.json`.\n- **A2 — guide + hook.** Full `init-agents`. Isolates whether wording alone sufficed.\n\n**Pre-registered bar, fixed before the first session runs:**\n\n| metric | A0 expectation | pass condition for A1 |\n|---|---|---|\n| sessions with ≥1 Graph or Brain call | low | **≥ 9/10** |\n| `graph_first_rate` | ~0 | **≥ 0.7** |\n| sessions that used a tool then abandoned it for grep | — | **≤ 3/10** |\n\nn=10 per arm is adequate *for an effect this large* — 0/10 vs 7/10 is Fisher exact p < 0.001 — and\nis not adequate for a small one. If the observed effect is small, the honest outcome is \"not\nestablished at this n\", not a wider search for a subgroup that worked.\n\nThe third row is the one that matters most and the one the earlier run exposed: first-call\ncompliance can rise while sustained usage stays flat.\n\n**Fairness rules, fixed in advance.** The graph session's warning is that their past A/Bs \"died of\nasymmetric prompts\", which matches this project's own benchmark doctrine, so these are not\nnegotiable mid-run:\n\n- **Symmetric instructions.** The task text is byte-identical across arms. The *only* difference is\n  the guide under test, which arrives through `init-agents` rather than the prompt. Test for any\n  sentence I am tempted to add: *would this help an arm that has no guide?* If yes, it goes in all\n  arms or none.\n- **Paired, symmetric gating.** A task counts only when every arm completed it; a task that fails\n  in one arm is dropped from all of them, not just the one that failed.\n- **Report the median and a bootstrap CI beside every rate**, with raw counts — not a mean alone.\n\n## Phase 4 — Ask every session what went wrong\n\nAfter each task, a follow-up turn in the same session: what was your first action, did you reach\nfor Brain or Graph, if not why not, what did the tool return, was it useful, what would have made\nyou use it. Collected verbatim.\n\nThis is the highest-yield channel available — it is what exposed the root cause in the first place\n(\"The agent guide says to start substantive tasks with `entire brain brief`… I skipped that\"). It is\nalso self-report, so it is used to *find* mechanisms, never to score the arms; scoring comes from\nthe three instrumented channels only.\n\n## Phase 5 — Loop\n\nEach round: read the feedback, find the top blocker, fix it in the stack (brain side) or hand it to\nthe graph session, re-run the affected arm, re-check against the same pre-registered bar. Stop when\nA1 or A2 clears the bar on a round where no new blocker class appeared, or when a round produces a\nblocker that is a product decision rather than a defect — which comes back to you rather than\ngetting guessed.\n\n**Candidate blockers already identified**, to check rather than assume: the `@`-import path (Probe A);\n`$CLAUDE_FILE_PATH` (Probe B); brain-only installs getting no Graph directive (`guide.go:128,130`);\n`ENTIRE_REPO_ROOT` mis-targeting; and MCP-first sessions having no Graph route at all, which no\nwording can fix.\n\n---\n\n## Cost, stated up front\n\nRoughly 32+ real `claude -p` sessions (2 probes + 3 arms × 10) plus a feedback turn each, at a\nlocate task's typical size. Order of a few million tokens per full round, and the loop may need more\nthan one round. Sonnet, small tasks, and arms re-run individually rather than all three each time.\nSay the word if you want a smaller first round — 5 per arm would still separate 0 from 0.7.\n\n## Verification\n\nThe deliverable is not \"the tests pass\" — it is a number against a bar fixed in advance:\n\n1. Phase 2 probes answered yes/no with the evidence inline.\n2. A table of A0 / A1 / A2 on all three metrics, with counts and a median + bootstrap CI beside\n   every rate, and the exact commands and both pinned binary versions that produced them.\n3. Every fix made during the loop carries a regression test verified by weakening — guard patched\n   compile-clean, test shown red, assertion quoted.\n4. The verbatim feedback, including anything that contradicts the result.\n5. If the bar is not cleared, that is reported as the finding, with what remains unfixed. A result\n   that says \"usage did not improve\" is a successful execution of this plan.\n\nNothing merges without your approval; PRs #329–#331 stay unmerged and this work stacks on #331.\n","tool_use_id":"toolu_01VcLaBN9MFABzg2tqFvsJn6"}]},"uuid":"b45cfac6-8550-49ff-ac3c-c7d93819a306","timestamp":"2026-10-03T13:58:05.510Z","toolUseResult":{"plan":"# Fix agent usage of Brain + Graph, and verify it in a clean environment\n\n## Context\n\nPRs #329–#331 (unmerged) fixed a text defect that plausibly suppressed tool usage: brain's\nshipped agent guide told agents they could skip the tools, and CI enforced that wording in both\ndirections. **Whether fixing it actually raises usage is unmeasured.**\n\nThe one experiment run so far is invalid, and it is worth being precise about why: the guide text\nwas injected into each agent's *prompt*. That tests whether the wording works once it is already\nin context — the thing nobody doubted. The real-world failure is that the guide *file* never\nchanges behaviour. Both arms used the tools 100% of the time, so there was no headroom to detect\nan increase, and n was 2 per arm.\n\nThis plan replaces that with a clean-environment measurement using real `claude -p` sessions, a\npre-registered success bar, and a fix/re-measure loop. Three exploration passes established the\nmechanics below; everything cited is `file:line` from source.\n\n### What is now established\n\n**Guide delivery (entire-brain).** `init-agents` writes `.entire/agent-guide.md`, root `AGENTS.md`,\nroot `CLAUDE.md` (carrying `@.entire/agent-guide.md`), and — new in #331 — a `PreToolUse` entry in\n`.claude/settings.json` (`internal/agentsetup/files.go:19,221,249`; `claudehooks.go:46`). `setup`\nwrites **none** of them (`setup.go` has no reference to any of those names), which confirms the\nreported \"AGENTS.md never appeared\" as intended behaviour, not a bug. There are almost no gates;\nthe only hard one is root resolution (`agent_setup.go:47`).\n\n**Two hazards in that path.** `ENTIRE_REPO_ROOT` outranks cwd (`env.go:23,73`), so a host that sets\nit can silently install into the outer repo; and the walk-up stops at the *innermost* `.git`\n(`migration.go:94-131`), so a nested clone captures the files. Also: the combined guide carrying\nthe Graph directive ships **only when both products are enabled** (`guide.go:128`) — a brain-only\ninstall gets `BrainGuide()` with no Graph instruction at all (`guide.go:130`).\n\n**Graph has no MCP server, by design.** No MCP/JSON-RPC dependency in `go.mod`, no server under\n`cmd/` or `internal/cli`, and `README.md:268` states it will not expose one. So Bash is the only\nroute to Graph, and the guide is correct to name a shell command — but an MCP-first harness has no\nGraph tool at all. This is a constraint to measure around, not a bug to fix.\n\n**The measurement already exists and is usable.** `entire graph stats` computes\n`graph_first_rate = GraphFirstSessions / SessionsWithLocate`\n(`entire-graph/internal/cli/stats.go:1183-1189,1236`) from local Claude Code JSONL transcripts in\n`~/.claude/projects/<repo-slug>/` (`:374-386`), and `--sessions-dir` / `--transcript` override that\npath — which is what makes a clean-env arm measurable. Two caveats that change the design:\n\n- **MCP calls are invisible to it.** Only Bash `entire graph <verb>` / `entire-graph <verb>` count\n  as graph calls (`:1048-1055`); `mcp__entire-brain__*` matches neither bucket. Brain usage\n  therefore needs its own measure.\n- **Pin the binary.** The withdrawn \"82.9M tokens saved\" figure was binary-version drift\n  (`scripts/entire-graph-statusline.sh:115`), and the graph-first rate runs in the same binary.\n\nThe exploration could not find the provenance of the quoted `332 / 96,987 (0.34%)` baseline — it is\nonly ever quoted, never derived. Treat it as context, not as this experiment's control.\n\n### The two unknowns that decide everything\n\nNeither is answerable from source, and both are cheap to settle:\n\n1. **Does the model actually receive the guide** through `CLAUDE.md`'s `@.entire/agent-guide.md`\n   import? If not, every wording fix in #331 is inert.\n2. **Is `$CLAUDE_FILE_PATH` populated** in a `PreToolUse` hook? #331's wiring passes\n   `--file \"$CLAUDE_FILE_PATH\"`; if that variable is not set, the hook runs with an empty path and\n   **my own hook is a no-op.**\n\nThese run first, as two single sessions, before any arm.\n\n---\n\n## Scope: entire-graph is OUT, and that is now settled\n\nThe decoy/ranking problem is the half that makes usage *persist* — 3 of 4 sessions used the tool\nexactly as instructed, got a decoy, and succeeded with grep instead. The\n`redesign-graph-cost-reduction` session answered, and it changes the picture:\n\n- **Sample-idf is already fixed, unmerged** — PR #291, also inside the launch-candidate stack\n  (#315 at `dd76b8fe`, and `0d92152d`). My earlier `internal/sem/search.go` citations were from a\n  stale build; the current code picks repo-wide DF when an exact corpus is known.\n- **I re-measured with their frozen binary and the decoy reproduces identically.**\n  `entire-graph-0d92152d` (sha256 `09f393fe…`): ranks 1–3 are\n  `brainBriefSemanticNeedsCurrentBoundary` 30.13 / `acquireSemanticIndexLock` 29.38 /\n  `runSemanticIngestTraces` 29.37 — the same scores to 2dp as the old binary, and **neither real\n  decider appears anywhere in the returned set.** So the idf fix does not move this case, which\n  isolates it to name-signal dominance rather than idf. Their follow-up sharpened it further: the\n  deciders being *absent from the returned set* makes this a **recall** failure, not merely an\n  ordering one — no reranking could rescue a candidate that was never returned.\n- **Name dominance is diagnosed, deliberately not patched ad hoc.** `searchNameCoverageWeight=10`\n  is intentionally not idf-weighted; their post-hoc study-6 found the name gap in 25/66 ranking\n  misses vs 16/123 hits, and a scoring emulator showed weight 0 rescuing 6 misses and losing 0\n  hits — emulator evidence only. The real test is a pre-registered \"study 7\" on 12 fresh repos\n  with a paraphrase stratum.\n\n**Codex holds the Go lease on entire-graph, and ranking changes go through that prereg.** So no\nentire-graph file gets touched here — tuning to my single case is exactly what the prereg exists to\nprevent, and they are right about that. Three commitments made to them and kept in this plan:\n\n1. The decoy query is **held out** — it goes into an evaluation set only, never a tuning set.\n2. The clean-env arms **pin graph to `0d92152d`**, so they measure their current state rather than\n   the stale installed symlink (which points at a dev checkout).\n3. The repro gets filed as a GitHub issue on `entireio/entire-graph` — recorded, no collision.\n\nOne finding handed back to them rather than acted on: `files_scanned=1855` but\n`files_content_read_during_preselection=1853`, with 12 `E_MINIFIED` partial failures in the same\noutput. If a deliberate skip counts as \"unread\" and thereby revokes the exact-corpus idf path, that\nis a defect in its own right. They are passing it, plus a suggestion to expose `idf_source` in\n`stats` so it is checkable from outside, to Codex.\n\n**Consequence for the bar below:** Graph ranking will still return this class of decoy during the\nexperiment. So the third pre-registered metric — used-then-abandoned — is measuring something this\nplan *cannot* fix. It is reported, not optimised, and if A1 clears rows 1–2 but fails row 3, the\nhonest conclusion is \"brain-side fix works; sustained usage is gated on graph ranking\", not a\nfailure of #331.\n\n---\n\n## Phase 1 — Harness (no model cost)\n\nBuild under `~/.claude/jobs/<job>/tmp/cleanenv/`, nothing inside `devenv/`.\n\n- **Pinned binaries.** Build `entire-brain` from the stack tip (`fix/agent-guide-directive-and-discovery`)\n  and record the commit; use the installed `entire-graph` and record its version. Every arm uses\n  the same two binaries.\n- **One fresh clone per arm**, each with no `.entire/`, no `CLAUDE.md`, no `AGENTS.md`, so arms\n  cannot contaminate each other. `ENTIRE_REPO_ROOT` explicitly unset in every arm (it outranks cwd).\n- **Instrumentation, three independent channels** — because no single one sees everything:\n  | channel | catches | why needed |\n  |---|---|---|\n  | PATH shim logging every `entire` call with a run id | all Brain + Graph **CLI** calls, in order | the only ordered record of what the agent actually ran |\n  | `entire graph stats --sessions-dir <arm>` | `graph_first_rate`, graph calls, exploration calls | the project's own metric, so before/after is comparable |\n  | transcript grep for `mcp__entire-brain__` | Brain **MCP** usage | invisible to `graph stats` |\n- **Isolated transcript dir per arm** so `--sessions-dir` reads only that arm's sessions.\n- A fixed task set (6–8 locate/explain/change tasks) written once and reused verbatim across arms.\n\n## Phase 2 — Settle the two unknowns (2 sessions)\n\n- **Probe A:** `claude -p \"quote the sentence in your instructions about your FIRST action\"` with cwd\n  in an `init-agents`-installed clone. Establishes whether the `@` import reaches the model.\n- **Probe B:** a session told to edit one file; check the shim log for a `hook pre-edit --file <path>`\n  call with a **non-empty** path. Establishes whether `$CLAUDE_FILE_PATH` works.\n\nIf Probe A fails, the fix is to inline the directive into `CLAUDE.md` rather than import it, and\neverything downstream re-runs. If Probe B fails, #331's hook wiring is corrected before the arms run.\n\n## Phase 3 — Arms, pre-registered\n\nThree arms, **n ≥ 10 sessions each**, same tasks, same binaries, fresh clone per arm:\n\n- **A0 — no guide.** `setup` only, no `init-agents`. The true baseline.\n- **A1 — guide only.** `init-agents`, hook entry removed from `.claude/settings.json`.\n- **A2 — guide + hook.** Full `init-agents`. Isolates whether wording alone sufficed.\n\n**Pre-registered bar, fixed before the first session runs:**\n\n| metric | A0 expectation | pass condition for A1 |\n|---|---|---|\n| sessions with ≥1 Graph or Brain call | low | **≥ 9/10** |\n| `graph_first_rate` | ~0 | **≥ 0.7** |\n| sessions that used a tool then abandoned it for grep | — | **≤ 3/10** |\n\nn=10 per arm is adequate *for an effect this large* — 0/10 vs 7/10 is Fisher exact p < 0.001 — and\nis not adequate for a small one. If the observed effect is small, the honest outcome is \"not\nestablished at this n\", not a wider search for a subgroup that worked.\n\nThe third row is the one that matters most and the one the earlier run exposed: first-call\ncompliance can rise while sustained usage stays flat.\n\n**Fairness rules, fixed in advance.** The graph session's warning is that their past A/Bs \"died of\nasymmetric prompts\", which matches this project's own benchmark doctrine, so these are not\nnegotiable mid-run:\n\n- **Symmetric instructions.** The task text is byte-identical across arms. The *only* difference is\n  the guide under test, which arrives through `init-agents` rather than the prompt. Test for any\n  sentence I am tempted to add: *would this help an arm that has no guide?* If yes, it goes in all\n  arms or none.\n- **Paired, symmetric gating.** A task counts only when every arm completed it; a task that fails\n  in one arm is dropped from all of them, not just the one that failed.\n- **Report the median and a bootstrap CI beside every rate**, with raw counts — not a mean alone.\n\n## Phase 4 — Ask every session what went wrong\n\nAfter each task, a follow-up turn in the same session: what was your first action, did you reach\nfor Brain or Graph, if not why not, what did the tool return, was it useful, what would have made\nyou use it. Collected verbatim.\n\nThis is the highest-yield channel available — it is what