{"parentUuid":"f67771fa-7242-44e5-9518-80ea192b2f84","isSidechain":false,"message":{"model":"claude-opus-5","id":"msg_011CffTdZcMY3tGdHXV5pFEB","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Round 2 is designed and the fixture defect is recorded. 28/30 — waiting on the last two sessions in A0/A1.\n\nWorth being explicit about what I just caught, because it changes how round 1 should be read:\n\n**My round-1 fixture had almost no relationships in it.** graphmark is 178 Python files with exactly *one* function having 3+ cross-file callers — standalone scripts, not an interconnected codebase. So round 1 could never have demonstrated tool value regardless of how I worded the tasks. The 100%-solved-at-identical-cost result is a property of the fixture I picked, not a measurement of the tool.\n\nI only found this by checking whether the round-2 tasks I was about to write had any targets. Measured density, exported functions with 4+ cross-file referencing files out of 40 sampled:\n\n| fixture | density |\n|---|---|\n| graphmark (round 1) | ~1 |\n| entire-api | 5 / 40 |\n| **cli (round 2)** | **10 / 40** |\n\nSo round 2 moves to `cli` — 1390 Go files, Go being graph's best-supported language — with a prep commit stripping its committed `.entire/` files identically in every arm.\n\nAnd it adds the metric that actually tests value, pre-registered now rather than after seeing results:\n\n> **solved on the relationship stratum: A1 > A0** — or the tool isn't pulling its weight on the question class it exists for.\n\nEverything else carries over unchanged: pinned binaries, byte-identical prompts that never name a tool, feedback as a separate `--resume` turn, paired gating, median + CI, and outcome beside usage."}],"container":null,"stop_reason":"end_turn","stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1713,"cache_read_input_tokens":677261,"output_tokens":537,"output_tokens_details":{"thinking_tokens":0},"ser