{"parentUuid":"38167a60-a31e-4e8a-b9e4-bcf7686e1cc4","isSidechain":false,"message":{"model":"claude-opus-5","id":"msg_011CffXG7aa4BYCjRGNoeMBH","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Round 2 is running clean, and I caught an arm-asymmetry of my own making before it could corrupt the result.\n\n**The confound:** cli commits its *own* `PreToolUse` capture hook. My B1 arm stripped the whole `PreToolUse` key, which removed cli's capture hook along with brain's pre-edit. B1 would have differed from B0/B2 in two ways while I reported it as one — the exact trap the graph session warned me about (\"our past A/Bs died of asymmetric prompts\"). Now rebuilt from B2's settings minus *only* the brain entry:\n\n```\nB0  PreToolUse = [cli capture]\nB1  PreToolUse = [cli capture]\nB2  PreToolUse = [cli capture, entire brain hook pre-edit]\n```\n\nFour contaminated sessions discarded, transcripts cleared, relaunched. B1 and B2 are already opening with `graph search` on the relationship tasks.\n\nOne thing worth noting about how round 2 differs in kind: the tasks now ask *\"which files call X, and what breaks if its signature changes\"* on symbols verified to have 5–8 cross-file callers. Grep can find the call sites but can't tell you what breaks. If the tool doesn't beat grep here, it doesn't beat it anywhere — and that's the finding I'll report either way.\n\nScoring will use file-set **recall** against derived ground truth for the relationship stratum, not a binary, so a partial answer scores partially."}],"container":null,"stop_reason":"end_turn","stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":986,"cache_read_input_tokens":699713,"output_tokens":487,"output_tokens_details":{"thinking_tokens":0},"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":986,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0},"inference_geo":"not_available","ite