nWorth recording: the first weakening found nothing, because every other test exercised only the detector — a perfect detector nothing consults looked identical to them.\" 2>&1 | tail -1\n\n$EB trail finding resolve 226 01M42MCX9CAMPMTQYAERT9N6SQ --repo gh/entireio/entire-brain -m \"FIXED in 8c4fcb8c — correct, and the inconsistency was in the same struct literal I had just written: I sanitised m.Memory and left the ids raw.\n\nConfirmed the path: facts_read_cmd.go:540 prints anchor.SessionID through valueOrUnset with %s, and valueOrUnset (env.go:737) returns the value unchanged — no quoting, no stripping.\n\nBOTH ids are sanitised, which is not optional: ReplacedBy is matched against ForeignID through byForeignID, so cleaning one and not the other would silently break every supersession whose id contained a stripped character. Stripping is disclosed through the same Unsupported channel as the text case.\n\nGuard: TestForeignIDsAreSanitisedLikeTheMemoryText feeds an escape sequence in id and a zero-width character in replaced_by, and asserts neither survives and that the stripping is reported. Reverting the id sanitisation turns it red.\" 2>&1 | tail -1\n\n$EB trail finding resolve 247 01M42N6V3PRADWNXEK1A4EB1R8 --repo gh/entireio/entire-brain -m \"FIXED in 85973b89 — correct, and it is a regression I introduced: the fix traded a clear error for a hang.\n\nThe read is now gated on hookStdinWouldBlock, true only when the reader IS the process's stdin and that stdin is a character device — using stdinIsTerminal (seed.go:1811) as suggested. The check is on the READER, not just the process: a harness always redirects stdin and is read, a test with its own reader is read regardless of what the runner did with os.Stdin, and only a person at a terminal gets \\\"--file is required\\\".\n\nStated plainly: the hang is NOT reproducible in-process, because it needs a real character device on stdin and \\`go test\\` does not provide one. So the guards are the predicate (every non-stdin reader is read; os.Stdin tracks stdinIsTerminal) plus a call-site assertion, and the test says it is weaker than a behavioural one rather than implying otherwise. Ungating the call site turns the call-site guard red.\" 2>&1 | tail -1\n\n$EB trail finding resolve 247 01M42N6V3D38AG3ZH7MQB30N39 --repo gh/entireio/entire-brain -m \"FIXED in 85973b89 — correct arithmetic and correct remedy; taken as prescribed.\n\ntotal summed c over the LIMIT 6 rows, so with more than six distinct kinds it was a partial sum of the top groups. The sting is exactly as described: that note exists to give an agent an accurate picture after an empty result, and a too-low total risks the same false root cause it was written to prevent — the mistake that prompted writing it.\n\ntotal is now its own SELECT count(*) FROM symbols, with LIMIT 6 kept only for the displayed list.\n\nOne addition beyond the finding, same defect class: the kind COUNT is its own query too, so a truncated list reads \\\"(6 of 9 kinds)\\\" rather than presenting six of nine as the whole set. Six or fewer kinds reads exactly as before, pinned so the fix cannot pass by inflating every count.\n\nGuards: TestKindInventoryTotalCountsEveryKindNotJustTheTopSix (nine kinds, total must be 9, displayed must stay 6), TestKindInventoryIsExactBelowTheDisplayLimit, and two on the note text. Restoring the partial sum turns two of them red at once.\" 2>&1 | tail -1","description":"Resolve the four new findings","timeout":1800000}},"serverClassifierRequest":"0ba15d23-4d7f-49a9-8da9-bd1265bd4f3d","apiBlockIndex":1,"requestId":"req_011CfgePpo4bbTe1DR9SfA24","type":"assistant","uuid":"b118aee3-e5b4-465c-ad39-544c5f5b55c3","timestamp":"2026-10-04T05:29:22.781Z","advisorModel":"claude-opus-5-5","effort":"xhigh","perTurnEffort":null,"session_id":"6ca2c8fb-c97b-4bf3-a992-0e3c6d1967e2","sessionKind":"bg","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/suhaan/devenv/brain-v1-guide","sessionId":"6ca2c8fb-c97b-4bf3-a992-0e3c6d1967e2