{"parentUuid":"316d9c3f-4a73-4dd9-964f-40323c7a702d","isSidechain":false,"message":{"model":"claude-opus-5","id":"msg_011CfgfygLNasVbvC4Gg7hcz","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_014WL9Pfw6hqnCPh4QP9YRh4","name":"Bash","input":{"command":"cd /Users/suhaan/devenv\nEB=/Users/suhaan/.claude/jobs/6ca2c8fb/tmp/entire-main\nR=\"gh/entireio/entire-brain\"\n\n$EB trail finding resolve 242 01M3ZZ7G70PG8FVFJ8RSAPXAWK --repo $R -m \"FIXED in ce7dce80 (stack tip) — correct in every particular, including the list of affected call sites. This was the most valuable finding of the round.\n\nThe uncapped wrapper threw away regressionScanCaps, so brain review and the workspace regressions/review surfaces could print an unqualified clean bill of health over a raw-history scan that hit a cap and never finished. regressionNoAnomaliesLine had been saying this correctly since the caps existed; reviewSummary and workspaceReviewSummary could not, because they never received the flag.\n\nBoth now take it and report PARTIAL. The workspace per-repo results carry history_truncated in JSON, so a machine reader need not parse prose — which is what those structs' own comments ask for. The multi-repo headline qualifies itself the same way it already qualifies coverage, and the comment there now names completeness as the same defect one layer in.\n\nTHE UNCAPPED WRAPPER IS DELETED. With both production callers converted it had none left, and leaving it is an invitation to discard the caps again. Its 25 test callers now call the capped form and discard the flag explicitly, which is fine: a test does not print a clean bill of health. A structural guard asserts the wrapper does not come back and that both summaries receive a real flag rather than a literal false.\n\nFixed at the STACK TIP rather than on #329's branch: #331 contains #329 and #330, the three merge in order, and rebasing the stack to place this on its own branch is churn with a real chance of breaking that order. Saying so plainly so the disposition is honest about where the code lives.\" 2>&1 | tail -1\n\n$EB trail finding resolve 242 01M3ZQDWHAVYPX6YT5F8ZE0C3M --repo $R -m \"FIXED in ce7dce80 (stack tip) — correct, and the sting is that it is the same rune-versus-byte confusion truncateStatusCause exists to fix, on the other operand. The function's whole doc comment is about that class of bug.\n\nThe reserve is now utf8.RuneCountInString(statusCauseEllipsis). It agrees with the old behaviour for ASCII \\\"...\\\" and stops over-reserving the moment the marker becomes a multi-byte character, which is one const edit away.\n\nGuards: a truncated cause must come back exactly statusCauseWidth runes (so limit plus marker is checked against the advertised width, not just the input), non-ASCII input is charged per rune, a cause that fits is returned untouched, and a structural check that the byte-counted expression is gone. Restoring len(statusCauseEllipsis) turns it red.\n\nFixed at the stack tip for the reason given on 01M3ZZ7G70PG8FVFJ8RSAPXAWK.\" 2>&1 | tail -1","description":"Resolve the caps and ellipsis findings","timeout":1800000},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":"tool_use","stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1377,"cache_read_input_tokens":446959,"output_tokens":1136,"output_tokens_details":{"thinking_tokens":0},"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":1377,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0},"inference_geo":"not_available","iterations":[{"input_tokens":2,"output_tokens":1136,"cache_read_input_tokens":446959,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1377,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":1377},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard","fallback_credit":null},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"wireToolInputs":{"too