{"parentUuid":"107effaf-327a-4e37-a7b9-ba382d5dbd22","isSidechain":false,"message":{"model":"claude-opus-5","id":"msg_011Cfgg2z82vdAuVJpEvPBba","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"`--agent-command` is a CLI flag only — line 2124's `AgentCommand` is a JSON *output* field. So the \"attacker\" is the user, and the switch only picks how the prompt reaches the binary they already chose. Let me disposition all four:"}],"container":null,"stop_reason":"tool_use","stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1419,"cache_read_input_tokens":451820,"output_tokens":2646,"output_tokens_details":{"thinking_tokens":482},"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":1419,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0},"inference_geo":"not_available","iterations":[{"input_tokens":2,"output_tokens":2646,"cache_read_input_tokens":451820,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1419,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":1419},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard","fallback_credit":null},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"serverClassifierRequest":"2717ce02-3838-49d2-9f47-ca9831ca8891","apiBlockIndex":1,"requestId":"req_011Cfgg2vuJneqiB8NVDv9ye","type":"assistant","uuid":"10ed8615-e488-4e42-90c4-3194a88ff28c","timestamp":"2026-10-04T05:50:38.375Z","advisorModel":"claude-opus-5-5","effort":"xhigh","perTurnEffort":null,"session_id":"6ca2c8fb-c97b-4bf3-a992-0e3c6d1967e2","sessionKind":"bg","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/suhaan/devenv/brain-v1-guide","sessionId":"6ca2c8fb-c97b-4bf3-a992-0e3c6d1967e2","version":"2.1.288","gitBranch":"main","slug":"dynamic-petting-globe"}
{"parentUuid":"10ed8615-e488-4e42-90c4-3194a88ff28c","isSidechain":false,"message":{"model":"claude-opus-5","id":"msg_011Cfgg2z82vdAuVJpEvPBba","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01YW9ywrcdrDF6nMhkAQSknQ","name":"Bash","input":{"command":"cd /Users/suhaan/devenv\nEB=/Users/suhaan/.claude/jobs/6ca2c8fb/tmp/entire-main\nR=\"gh/entireio/entire-brain\"\n\n$EB trail finding dismiss 246 01M400QGF9HRRJVD60KHBYVZ20 --repo $R -m \"REFUTED on threat model, and the prescribed remedy would break a supported use.\n\nWHO SUPPLIES argv[0]: --agent-command is a CLI flag and nothing else. It is registered with StringArrayVar at distill_cmd.go:653, facts_admin_cmd.go:629 and facts_eval_gen.go:121, and there is no config or environment path to it. The AgentCommand field at distill_cmd.go:2124 is a json output field on a report struct, not an input. So the \\\"attacker supplying --agent-command\\\" is the person already holding the command line, who can run any binary directly.\n\nWHAT THE SWITCH DECIDES: not what to execute. argv[0] runs either way. The switch picks only HOW THE PROMPT IS DELIVERED to the binary the caller already named — claude gets --system-prompt-file pointing at a 0600 temp file, codex gets it composed onto stdin, anything else passes through untouched. Matching \\\"claude\\\" buys an attacker a mode-0600 file containing the user's own prompt, readable by a process already running as that user. There is no privilege gain to obtain.\n\nWHY THE REMEDY IS WORSE: the switch already IS an allowlist of handled agents. Resolving argv[0] and demanding it match a known binary path would reject a wrapper script named claude — which the comment at distill_cmd.go:2315 names as a supported case (\\\"a --agent-command wrapper\\\"). The change would break a documented use to prevent a user from choosing their own binary.\n\nTHE ONE CONDITION THAT WOULD CHANGE THIS: if --agent-command ever becomes settable from repo-local configuration, then repo content would select an executable and this stops being a user choice. At that point the problem is arbitrary execution from repo content, far larger than basename classification, and it needs a decision rather than a line edit. Recorded here so the reasoning is on the record if that day comes.\" 2>&1 | tail -1\n\n$EB trail finding resolve 246 01M400QGEVTWJN6RJPZZ2C2J3H --repo $R -m \"FIXED in ce7dce80 (stack tip) — correct, and the prescribed fix is exactly what was applied: read the index once and take both the contents and the FileInfo from that single operation.\n\nos.ReadFile followed by os.Stat on the path were two separate lookups, so a concurrent git could replace the index between them and the scratch copy would carry one version's bytes with another version's mtime. That is precisely the pairing the racily-clean logic depends on being consistent — the mtime is copied onto the scratch index so git can compare tracked files against it, and a mismatched pair makes that comparison meaningless.\n\nNow one os.Open, with Stat() and io.ReadAll from the same handle. git writes the index by rename, so an open file description keeps the inode it opened: the contents and the FileInfo are guaranteed to describe the same version however many times the path is replaced afterwards.\n\nFixed at the stack tip for the reason given on 01M3ZZ7G70PG8FVFJ8RSAPXAWK.\" 2>&1 | tail -1\n\n$EB trail finding resolve 246 01M3ZWQG0QMNF93QK2FKKR1VW0 --repo $R -m \"PARTLY FIXED in ce7dce80, and the remainder is inherent — worth separating, because the two halves have different answers.\n\nFIXED: the half that was a real inconsistency. The contents and the mtime came from two separate lookups (os.ReadFile then os.Stat on the path), so the copy could carry one version's bytes with another version's mtime. Both now come from a single os.Open handle; because git replaces the index by rename, that handle keeps its inode and the pair is guaranteed consistent. Same fix as 01M400QGEVTWJN6RJPZZ2C2J3H, which states it more precisely.\n\nNOT FIXED, BY DESIGN: the scratch copy can still be stale relative to a write that lands after it is taken. That is not a defect in the mechanism, it IS the mechanism. The function exists to answer a diff without opening .git/index for write, and any lock-free read is a snapshot as of some instant. Closing that window requires taking the lock, which is the contention the function was written to avoid — see its comment and the measured table above it.\n\nWhat the snapshot guarantees is a coherent answer as of one point in time, not an answer that cannot be overtaken. A caller needing the stronger property has to hold the lock, and `brain brief` (the caller this was written for) does not.\" 2>&1 | tail -1\n\n$EB trail finding resolve 246 01M400QGFEY7KACEDRTT6C2BXW --repo $R -m \"FIXED in ce7dce80 (stack tip) — taken even though the finding itself calls the depth unlikely, because the iterative form costs nothing and \\\"short in practice\\\" is not a bound.\n\nfindRoot recursed once per link in the parent chain while compressing it. Union by smaller id keeps chains short in practice, which is why it has not bitten. It is still not a limit.\n\nNow iterative: walk to the root collecting the path, then point every node on it straight at the root. Same path compression, same results, bounded stack.\n\nGuard: a 50,000-deep chain resolves, and resolves to the same root from every node on it. A recursive version would pass that at small depth — the point is that it passes at a depth that was previously a stack risk. Plus a structural check that the recursive closure form is gone.\n\nFixed at the stack tip for the reason given on 01M3ZZ7G70PG8FVFJ8RSAPXAWK.\" 2>&1 | tail -1","description":"Disposition all four 330 findings","timeout":1800000},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":"tool_use","stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1419,"cache_read_in