{"host":"github.com","remote":"entireio/entire-brain","visibility":"public"},{"host":"github.com","remote":"entireio/entire-graph","visibility":"public"},{"host":"github.com","remote":"entirehq/entiredb","visibility":"private"},{"host":"github.com","remote":"entireio/cli","visibility":"public"},{"host":"github.com","remote":"entirehq/entire.io","visibility":"private"}]}},"live_cwd":"/Users/suhaan/devenv/brain-v1-guide","platform":"macos"}},"sourceToolAssistantUUID":"b26e6c24-4908-4e1b-be78-ada7c7a0748e","session_id":"6ca2c8fb-c97b-4bf3-a992-0e3c6d1967e2","sessionKind":"bg","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/suhaan/devenv/brain-v1-guide","sessionId":"6ca2c8fb-c97b-4bf3-a992-0e3c6d1967e2","version":"2.1.288","gitBranch":"main","slug":"dynamic-petting-globe"}
{"parentUuid":"8c70bf9c-5624-4918-b1e3-357688e501fd","isSidechain":false,"attachment":{"type":"total_tokens_reminder","text":"<total_tokens>14992267 tokens left</total_tokens>"},"type":"attachment","uuid":"de61c741-b2f1-4f61-bc4a-19dd6a0983a4","timestamp":"2026-10-04T20:59:02.590Z","rendered":[{"content":"<system-reminder>\n<total_tokens>14992267 tokens left</total_tokens>\n</system-reminder>"}],"renderedRole":"system","session_id":"6ca2c8fb-c97b-4bf3-a992-0e3c6d1967e2","sessionKind":"bg","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/suhaan/devenv/brain-v1-guide","sessionId":"6ca2c8fb-c97b-4bf3-a992-0e3c6d1967e2","version":"2.1.288","gitBranch":"main","slug":"dynamic-petting-globe"}
{"parentUuid":"de61c741-b2f1-4f61-bc4a-19dd6a0983a4","isSidechain":false,"message":{"model":"claude-opus-5","id":"msg_011CfhsKU4mzVr9xejtNVZwB","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Re-verified all five. Here's what they are and where each stands.\n\n## The bugs, current status\n\n**1. `inspect impact --symbol ValidateToken` → `unknown flag: --symbol`** — still broken.\n\nThis is a **docs bug, not a product bug**, and I checked before concluding that: `impact`'s siblings `context` and `tests` are positional too. Adding `--symbol` would make `impact` the odd one out. The docs should say `entire brain inspect impact ValidateToken`.\n\n**2. `init-agents` missing from the setup instructions** — still missing. The Overview teaches `setup` → `status` and never mentions the step that makes an agent actually use Brain. Brain's own output lists it *first* under Next.\n\n**3. Partial clone silently kills the code index** — **already fixed in code** (`6041e637`). Brain now names the cause and a verified remedy. The docs still don't mention it under \"Before You Start\".\n\n**4. \"code index is unavailable\" understates it** — `refresh --semantic` *errors* without Graph, it doesn't quietly degrade. Still worded softly.\n\n**5. `--path` must have three parts** — still unstated. `--path project.deploy` is rejected: *\"not a valid taxonomy path (category.subcategory.type)\"*. The examples are all valid; the rule never appears.\n\n## Why I can't fix 1, 2, 4, 5\n\n**The docs source isn't in this workspace.** I searched `entire.io/`, `entire.io/docs/`, and every `.mdx` in devenv — the brain guide pages aren't there. They're published from a separate repo (the `<Tabs>`/`<Note>` components are Mintlify).\n\nSo these are four one-line edits I have no file to make them in. **Point me at the docs repo and I'll fix all four in a few minutes** — they're small and I have the exact replacement text in the artifact.\n\nI deliberately did *not* \"fix\" #1 by adding a `--symbol` flag to the product. That would bend working, consistent code to match a typo and add permanent API surface. The doc is wrong; the doc should change.\n\n---\n\nSeparately, `/court` is built — `.claude/skills/court/SKILL.md`, uncommitted. Two forced-position advocates, 12 independent jurors with one lens each, a judge that rules on the strongest surviving objection rather than averaging votes, and a loop that stops when the vote trend goes flat. A unanimous round-1 acquittal is treated as suspicious rather than a win."}],"container":null,"stop_reason":"end_turn","stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":993,"cache_read_input_tokens":818927,"output_tokens":820,"output_tokens_details":{"thinking_tokens":0},"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":993},"inference_geo":"not_available","iterations":[{"input_tokens":2,"output_tokens":820,"cache_read_input_tokens":818927,"cache_creation_input_tokens":993,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":993,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard","fallback_credit":null},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"serverClassifierRequest":"51ce02ba-e953-4d08-bf19-ea2f4fc8a3c0","apiBlockIndex":0,"requestId":"req_011CfhsKTLsCEppWJMEuqV8B","type":"assistant","uuid":"725253b8-53a2-4c49-b8dc-d15a3d2df434","timestamp":"2026-10-04T20:59:14.250Z","advisorModel":"claude-opus-5-5","effort":"xhigh","perTurnEffort":null,"session_id":"6ca2c8fb-c97b-4bf3-a992-0e3c6d1967e2","sessionKind":"bg","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/suhaan/devenv/brain-v1-guide","sessionId":"6ca2c8fb-c97b-4bf3-a992-0e3c6d1967e2","version":"2.1.288","gitBranch":"main","slug":"dynamic-petting-globe"}
{"parentUuid":"725253b8-53a2-4c49-b8dc-d15a3d2df434","isSidechain":false,"type":"system","subtype":"stop_hook_summary","hookCount":2,"hookInfos":[{"command":"$CLAUDE_PROJECT_DIR/.claude/hooks/claude-bridge-wake.py"},{"command":"node \"${CLAUDE_PLUGIN_ROOT}/scripts/stop-review-gate-hook.mjs\"","durationMs":50}],"hookErrors":[],"hookAdditionalContext":[],"preventedContinuation":false,"stopReason":"","hasOutput":false,"level":"suggestion","timestamp":"2026-10-04T20:59:16.328Z","uuid":"a5040e51-e706-4bcc-a110-02db97e46576","toolUseID":"290fb91b-6b86-49bf-956a-7a4e1fcbde92","session_id":"6ca2c8fb-c97b-4bf3-a992-0e3c6d1967e2","sessionKind":"bg","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/suhaan/devenv/brain-v1-guide","sessionId":"6ca2c8fb-c97b-4bf3-a992-0e3c6d1967e2","version":"2.1.288","gitBranch":"main","slug":"dynamic-petting-globe"}
{"parentUuid":"a5040e51-e706-4bcc-a110-02db97e46576","isSidechain":false,"type":"system","subtype":"turn_duration","durationMs":109504,"messageCount":8313,"timestamp":"2026-10-04T20:59:16.333Z","uuid":"2de4ae3c-0cbb-4cec-82ca-df9e9cb9ad67","isMeta":false,"sessionKind":"bg","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/suhaan/devenv/brain-v1-guide","sessionId":"6ca2c8fb-c97b-4bf3-a992-0e3c6d1967e2","version":"2.1.288","gitBranch":"main","slug":"dynamic-petting-globe"}
{"parentUuid":"2de4ae3c-0cbb-4cec-82ca-df9e9cb9ad67","isSidechain":false,"promptId":"9b46df3f-00e5-46c1-ab32-eed9b8e1a5f6","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":"\n\n<pasted_content id=\"3b25\">\n---\ntitle: Overview\ndescription: Give your coding agents shared memory of your project, past decisions, and lessons learned.\n---\n\n## What Is Brain?\n\nEntire Brain is a memory layer for your Git repository. It brings together saved agent sessions, checkpoints, project facts, documentation, and code structure from [Entire Graph](/guides/graph/overview).\n\nGraph maps how your code connects while Brain adds the context behind it: why a decision was made, what was tried before, and what your team learned.\n\nThat memory belongs to the project and different coding agents can use it. For example,you can move from Claude Code to Codex and give the next agent context from earlier work.\n\n## How Does It Help?\n\nBrain is great for team collaboration and individual work when you are context switching across tasks and agents. It helps you spend less time explaining your project and repeating earlier research. A new agent session can start with the decisions, rules, and lessons that has already been recorded.\n\nFor example, before changing how failed requests are retried, you can ask Brain for earlier decisions about retries. Your agent can use that context to understand the current approach before editing the code.\n\n## What Can You Do with Brain?\n\n- Understand a project to get an overview of its structure and how it works.\n- Prepare for a task and find relevant decisions, code locations, and possible tests.\n- Find earlier work by searching through saved facts, history, and documentation.\n- Record a project rule or decision for future sessions.\n- Use memory to browse what Brain knows in a local UI.\n\n\n## Before You Start\n\nUse a local Git repository with at least one commit. Install the [Entire CLI](/installation) and Git 2.36 or later.\n\nTo give Brain access to future agent sessions, [enable Entire in your repository](/quickstart). Brain can also work from code, docs, and Git history when no saved sessions are available and make sure to commit your intended changes before setup.\n\n### Install Brain\n\nRun this once on your machine:\n\n```bash\ncurl -fsSL https://raw.githubusercontent.com/entireio/entire-brain/main/scripts/get-brain.sh | bash\n```\n\nThe installer downloads a prebuilt binary, checks its checksum, and registers the `entire brain` command.\n\nInstall Graph for code analysis:\n\n```bash\nentire plugin install graph\n```\n\nWithout Graph, Brain can still use sessions, docs, and facts, but its code index is unavailable.\n\n### Set Up Your Repository\n\nFrom your repository's root, run:\n\n```bash\nentire brain setup\nentire brain status\n```\n\nSetup builds the repository's memory. Status shows what is ready and what needs attention.\n\n<Note>\n  By default, setup also extracts facts from past sessions and installs a background\n  watcher on supported macOS and Linux systems. Fact extraction uses an available\n  coding agent, can send session content to its model provider, and can incur token\n  charges. The watcher can continue extracting facts over time.\n\n  To skip the initial fact extraction and watcher installation, use\n  `entire brain setup --no-backfill --no-daemon` instead.\n</Note>\n\n\n## How to Use Brain\n\nFor a guided walkthrough, follow the [Brain Quickstart](/guides/brain/quickstart).\n\nUse Brain from the CLI or UI after setup, or use the agent prompt to have your agent set it up for you.\n\n<Tabs>\n  <Tab title=\"CLI\" icon=\"terminal\">\n    Run these commands from your repository.\n\n    Get a project overview:\n\n    ```bash\n    entire brain overview\n    ```\n\n    Get context for a task:\n\n    ```bash\n    entire brain brief \"update the retry policy\"\n    ```\n\n    Search earlier decisions:\n\n    ```bash\n    entire brain query \"why did we choose this retry policy\"\n    ```\n\n    Save a fact, then find it again. Replace the example with a rule that is true for your project:\n\n    ```bash\n    entire brain remember \"Retry failed requests at most three times.\" --path constraints.retry.policy\n    entire brain recall \"retry policy\"\n    ```\n\n    Check whether memory is current:\n\n    ```bash\n    entire brain status\n    ```\n\n    See the [command reference](https://github.com/entireio/entire-brain/blob/main/docs/reference.md) for more options.\n  </Tab>\n\n  <Tab title=\"UI\" icon=\"browser\">\n    Open Brain's local browser view from your repository:\n\n    ```bash\n    entire brain viz\n    ```\n\n    Explore the graph to see code relationships, saved facts, sessions, history, and docs. This view runs on your machine and is read-only.\n\n    <div style={{ position: \"relative\", paddingTop: \"56.25%\" }}>\n      <iframe\n        src=\"https://customer-0x5hsk8lh27fm00g.cloudflarestream.com/988f4fb499040d1c5b3cc2417046e720/iframe\"\n        title=\"Explore Brain in the UI\"\n        loading=\"lazy\"\n        style={{\n          border: \"none\",\n          position: \"absolute\",\n          top: 0,\n          left: 0,\n          height: \"100%\",\n          width: \"100%\",\n        }}\n        allow=\"accelerometer; gyroscope; autoplay; encrypted-media; picture-in-picture;\"\n        allowFullScreen\n      />\n    </div>\n\n    If you prefer to stay in your terminal, open the dashboard:\n\n    ```bash\n    entire brain dash\n    ```\n\n    ![Brain terminal dashboard showing memory sources, session counts, saved facts, and working tree status.](/images/brain-terminal-dashboard.png)\n\n    Use it to browse status, facts, sessions, history, and code records. Both views let you explore existing memory without making model calls.\n\n  </Tab>\n\n  <Tab title=\"Agent Prompt\" icon=\"robot\">\n    To have your agent set up Brain for you, open it in your repository and send this prompt:\n\n    ```text\n    Set up Entire Brain in this repository and start using it for our work.\n\n    Check that the Entire CLI, Git 2.36 or later, and Entire Graph are\n    installed. Install any missing tools using the official instructions\n    at https://github.com/entireio/entire-brain#install.\n\n    Make sure this is a Git repository with at least one commit. If there\n    are uncommitted changes that block setup, tell me what needs attention.\n\n    Run `entire brain setup` to build the project's memory, then run\n    `entire brain init-agents` to add the agent instructions. Preserve my\n    existing instructions outside the managed blocks.\n\n    Run `entire brain status --verbose` and explain anything missing or\n    out of date. Read `.entire/agent-guide.md`, then run\n    `entire brain overview` and summarize what Brain knows about this project.\n\n    For my next task, use `entire brain brief` to gather relevant context\n    before making changes. Check the supporting sources and use Entire\n    Graph to find and inspect the code.\n    ```\n\n  </Tab>\n</Tabs>\n\n</pasted_content id=\"3b25\">\n\n||| \n\n<pasted_content id=\"3b25\">\n---\ntitle: Getting started with Brain\nsidebarTitle: Brain Use Cases\ndescription: Learn what you can do with Brain and find the right command for each task.\n---\n\nYou can use Brain to find earlier decisions, save lessons, and give your agent context before it starts work. Below are some ways to use Brain in your project.\n\n[Install Brain and set up your repository](/guides/brain/overview#before-you-start) first. Run these commands from the repository, and replace the example questions and rules with your own.\n\n## Get to Know a Project\n\nIf you are joining a project or returning after a break, you can ask Brain for an overview of its structure, tools, and conventions.\n\n```bash\nentire brain overview\n```\n\n## Get Context Before Starting a Task\n\nDescribe what you want to build or change. Brain gathers relevant facts, earlier work, code locations, and possible tests into a brief.\n\n```bash\nentire brain brief \"add rate limiting to the upload endpoint\"\n```\n\nYou can now use the brief to plan your work or give your coding agent a starting point.\n\n## Find Out Why a Decision Was Made\n\nSearch earlier work before reopening a decision or trying an approach your team has already explored.\n\n```bash\nentire brain query \"why did we remove the queue abstraction\"\n```\n\nThis helps you read the supporting sources to understand the decision and whether it still applies.\n\n## Search for a Topic\n\nIf you are searching for a topic and know the keyword, you can use it with the command below:\n\n```bash\nentire brain query --keyword \"rate limiter\"\n```\n\nAnd if you know the problem but not the exact words, you can search by meaning:\n\n```bash\nentire brain query --semantic \"handling temporary request failures\"\n```\n\nWithout either flag, `query` combines keyword and meaning-based search. Make sure to use only one of these flags at a time.\n\n## Save a Rule for Future Work\n\nYou can also record a decision or lesson so another session can find it. It is advisable to only save rules that are true for your project.\n\n```bash\nentire brain remember \"Retry failed requests at most three times.\" --path constraints.retry.policy\n```\n\nThe path flag groups the fact under a topic. This lets you save the fact without asking an agent to choose a category.\n\n## Look Up Saved Rules\n\nBefore changing behavior, you can check what your project has already recorded about it.\n\n```bash\nentire brain recall \"retry policy\"\n```\n\nUse `recall` for saved facts and `query` for a broader search across facts, history, and docs.\n\n## Check What a Code Change Could Affect\n\nWith Entire Graph installed, inspect the impact of changing a function or type. Replace `ValidateToken` with a symbol from your code.\n\n```bash\nentire brain inspect impact --symbol ValidateToken\n```\n\nYou can also inspect changes since the last index:\n\n```bash\nentire brain inspect changes\n```\n\n## Check Whether Memory Is Current\n\nYou can see which sources are available, missing, or out of date, using:\n\n```bash\nentire brain status --verbose\n```\n\nCheck whether saved facts still point to matching sources:\n\n```bash\nentire brain verify\n```\n\nA fact entered by hand can show `unverifiable-here` because it has no source reference. Verification checks references, not whether a fact is correct.\n\n\n</pasted_content id=\"3b25\">\n\n|||  - make a arfitact listing all errros int he setup doc/docs, and what to fix"},"uuid":"2293c15a-942e-4bc6-8b12-a0ff3f3e195a","timestamp":"2026-10-04T20:59:32.702Z","permissionMode":"auto","origin":{"kind":"human"},"promptSource":"typed","turnOrigin":"human","sessionKind":"bg","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/suhaan/devenv/brain-v1-guide","sessionId":"6ca2c8fb-c97b-4bf3-a992-0e3c6d1967e2","version":"2.1.288","gitBranch":"main","slug":"dynamic-petting-globe"}
