{"parentUuid":"4746aef3-c0d5-4780-9369-0bc894187efe","isSidechain":false,"message":{"model":"claude-opus-5","id":"msg_011Cfhx1JfKJiLJhpfzauDb8","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"That's why `/brain-memory` was unknown: `CLAUDE_CODE_PLUGIN_DIRS` is read when a session starts, and I added it to settings *after* this one was already running. `/reload-plugins` only reloads folders the session already knew about — it never saw the new one. That's on me; I verified the settings route on a fresh `claude -p` and then assumed a reload would pick it up mid-session.\n\nThe supported path for a live session is `~/.claude/dev-mods/<session-id>/`, and the mod is now written there as real files, byte-identical to the canonical copy, validating clean with 16/16 tests passing from that location.\n\n**When this turn ends you should get a prompt: \"Enable hot reloading for this session?\" — choose \"Enable for this session\".** The mod loads immediately after, and `/brain-memory` opens the pane. Each later edit reloads on the turn that makes it.\n\nTwo things worth knowing:\n\n- The `settings.json` entry still stands and covers **new** sessions, so you don't need to repeat this. The dev-mods copy is scoped to this session id only.\n- If you ever `--resume` *this* session id after a restart, both copies could load and you'd see the command twice. Deleting `~/.claude/dev-mods/6ca2c8fb-c97b-4bf3-a992-0e3c6d1967e2/` clears that — it's scratch.\n\nCanonical mod: `/Users/suhaan/devenv/.claude/mods/brain-memory/`. If the prompt doesn't appear, `claude --plugin-dir /Users/suhaan/devenv/.claude/mods/brain-memory` loads it in a fresh terminal session with no setup at all."}],"container":null,"stop_reason":"end_turn","stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1044,"cache_read_input_tokens":271902,"output_tokens":967,"output_tokens_details":{"thinking_tokens":409},"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"w