{"type":"queue-operation","operation":"enqueue","timestamp":"2026-10-06T22:27:53.678Z","sessionId":"6ca2c8fb-c97b-4bf3-a992-0e3c6d1967e2","content":"<task-notification>\n<task-id>a5f8e1f417e9b2101</task-id>\n<tool-use-id>REDACTED</tool-use-id>\n<output-file>/private/tmp/claude-501/-Users-suhaan-devenv/6ca2c8fb-c97b-4bf3-a992-0e3c6d1967e2/tasks/a5f8e1f417e9b2101.output</output-file>\n<status>completed</status>\n<summary>Agent \"Audit truncation discoverability\" finished</summary>\n<note>A task-notification fires each time this agent stops with no live background children of its own. The user can send it another message and resume it, so the same task-id may notify more than once.</note>\n<result>1. LOW | The marker is at the document root for every row-trimmed result. Object root: `root[response_truncated]` and `root[response_truncation]` are set on the root map (mcp_response_budget.go:493-496). Array root: wrapped as `{items, response_truncated, response_truncation}` (mcp_response_budget.go:498-502). Caveat: `dropped_from[].path` is a dotted nested path (mcp_response_budget.go:337-346, e.g. `impact.relations`), so the reader has to follow it. Retrieval tools (brain_query/search/vsearch) also stamp `ResponseTruncated` on the last surviving row (retrieval_transport.go:313, 636), alongside the root flag (309, 691). Fix: none needed for placement. Optional: put a one-line summary string at the root, not only inside `response_truncation`.\n\n2. HIGH | The marker covers only the 128 KiB byte-budget trim (and the retrieval-surface byte trim). Every other cap returns a short result with no marker:\n   - Per-tool `limit` default, tools with `limit` or `depth` (mcp.go:1062-1073; the 20 default and the per-tool overrides are set there). Affects brain_code/search_code (10), brain_context (5), brain_tests (3), brain_impact, brain_changes/detect_changes, brain_search_graph, brain_query_graph, brain_dead_code, brain_regressions/review (+ workspace variants), brain_workspace_graph, brain_boundaries, brain_trace_path (depth 4), brain_brief.\n   - Pagination object for brain_code and brain_context is `{limit, offset, count=len(results)}` only (semantic.go:4456-4460, built at 3450 and 3541). No `total`, `has_more` or `next_offset`, so `count==limit` is the agent's only hint.\n   - SQL `LIMIT` and slice cuts in graph tools (semantic_graph.go:340-352 graphRankMap, 635-636 queryGraphRelations, 962 dead_code) are silent.\n   - Snippet byte/line cap: `semanticContent.Truncated` is `omitempty` (semantic.go:3299) and nested per row (5651, from 5673-5684). It is visible only per row, never at the root.\n   - agent_surface.go:5196: `Truncated: len(matches) &gt;= limit` is a heuristic that fires on exact fit.\n   - Provider-side or CLI-level caps: unverified, not enumerated.\n   Fix: have the semantic runners fetch limit+1 rows and emit `has_more` and `truncated_by_limit`. Add a root-level `limit_applied` to `semanticPage`. Hoist per-row snippet `truncated` to a root flag.\n\n3. MED | Rewriting counts destroys the truncation evidence only where the `response_truncation` record is absent. `mcpRowCountRepair` rewrites `count`, `returned`, `result_count` and `returned_count` (and the same keys inside `pagination`) to the kept row count (mcp_response_budget.go:70, 366-401). When trimming happened, the record keeps `{returned,total}` (343-346), so the evidence survives. Residual risks:\n   - `total` is the pre-trim count, already capped by `limit` (stated in the instructions), so the true corpus size is never available.\n   - `count` now disagrees with the pre-trim count and the agent loses the \"count==limit means maybe more\" signal. This is minor.\n   - The repair is a heuristic: it rewrites any ancestor count that equals the list length, so an unrelated count could be changed by coincidence. Unverified.\n   Fix: add a `count_before_trim` field, or have the repair leave the count and put the original in the record.\n\n4. MED | The error is semi-actionable (mcp_response_budget.go:138-140): `\"&lt;tool&gt; produced a N byte result, over the 131072 byte MCP response budget (...frame limit...); it could not be reduced by dropping result rows, so narrow the request (lower limit, or drop details)\"`. Defects:\n   - It is generic and does not name the tool's actual args.\n   - It suggests `limit` and `details`, but tools like brain_get, brain_multi_get, brain_get_graph_schema and brain_status have no `limit` or `details` arg, so the advice does not apply.\n   - It is returned as an RPC error, not a tool result with `isError`. Whether it surfaces as -32000 is unverified.\n   Fix: build the hint from the tool's inputSchema properties (list the real size-bounding args). For `ids` (brain_multi_get), say \"request fewer ids\".\n\n5. HIGH | The contract is visible to an agent only in the server `instructions` string (mcp.go:668, text built at mcp_response_budget.go:52-63), plus a one-line note on integer args built via `integerArg` (mcp.go:729, mcp_response_budget.go:48). That note says \"removable rows may be trimmed with response_truncated; otherwise errors\" but never mentions the `response_truncation` record, root placement, or that `total` is capped. Gaps:\n   - No mention in `.entire/agent-guide.md` or agent_surface.go. `grep truncat|response_` there finds only unrelated items. docs/semantic_mcp_guide.md:263 does mention `response_truncated`, but that file is repo docs, not an agent surface.\n   - Tools with custom limit schemas (mcp.go:794 conversation concept limit, 856 brain_get outline limit) bypass `integerArg`, so they get no note.\n   - Tools with no size-bounding arg (brain_status, brain_get, multi_get, get_graph_schema) get no note at all.\n   - Clients that drop server instructions see only the per-arg note, and only on tools that use it.\n   Fix: put one full sentence in every tool description, or a shared suffix: \"Check root `response_truncated`; the details are in `response_truncation`\". Add the same text to the agent-guide generator. Make `integerArg` mention `response_truncation`.\n\n6. MED | The advertised budget in the instructions I was shown is 32768 bytes. This checkout has `mcpToolResponseMaxBytes = 128 * 1024` = 131072 (mcp_response_budget.go:16). The reachable and deployed brain-install-ux build is not the same as the one I was shown, so one of them is stale. The code comments, the retrieval surface and docs/semantic_mcp_guide.md:263 all say 128 KiB. Fix: confirm which binary the MCP server runs, then align the instructions text with the constant. The text is generated from the constant, so a mismatch means a stale binary. Unverified which side is stale.\n\n7. LOW | Non-JSON results, JSON with no row lists, and rows nested deeper than 6 levels cannot be trimmed, so they fall through to the error in item 4 (mcp_response_budget.go:149-155, mcpResponseTrimMaxDepth=6 at 28). Arrays inside rows are never trimmed, so one oversized row can force the error even when the list has many rows. Fix: surface which list or row is the blocker in the error message.</result>\n<usage><subagent_tokens>97563</subagent_tokens><tool_uses>18</tool_uses><duration_ms>79579</duration_ms></usage>\n</task-notification>"}
