rds — not the internal cause |\n| `anchor` | `file:line` |\n| `fix` | one line |\n| `status` | `open` → `fixed` → `verified` → (`regressed`) |\n| `check` | the exact command that proves it, re-runnable by anyone |\n| `last_verified` | date + how (weakening / fresh-env / both) |\n\n`symptom` is phrased from the outside on purpose: a list written in internal vocabulary\n(`memory_overlay`, `dirty_worktree`) re-creates the very problem we are fixing.\n\n### 2. The loop\n\nPick highest-severity `open` → fix → **weakening-verify** → **fresh-env verify** → set `verified`\nwith date and command. One PR per small batch; brain's constraint is review throughput, not\nauthorship (13 merged last week while 6 finished PRs queued), so batches stay reviewable.\n\n### 3. Fresh-env harness — `scripts/freshenv-check.sh`, committed\n\nEach check runs in a throwaway git repo with its own `XDG_DATA_HOME`/config, so no existing brain,\nplugin registry or cache leaks in. Committed so any session or subagent can run it unchanged.\n\n**Stated limit, up front:** this cannot catch \"the install instructions are wrong\", because the\nbinaries are already on the machine. Install-step defects need a real cold start and are marked\n`unverifiable-here` rather than silently passed. A harness that cannot fail the case it is\npointed at reports success — that failure mode has already bitten this work three times today.\n\n### 4. Regression sweep\n\nPeriodically, in a **fresh session**, a subagent re-runs the whole harness and flips any failing\nrow to `regressed`. Fresh matters: a session that made a fix is the worst judge of whether it\nholds.\n\n---\n\n## Verification bar (per row, no exceptions)\n\n1. **A test that fails on a compile-clean revert**, with the failing assertion quoted. Not an extra\n   step — CONTRIBUTING.md item 3 already requires it, and the PR must carry the red output.\n2. **A fresh-env check** that reproduces the user-visible symptom and shows it gone.\n3. **A negative control**